00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:30.420
So, okay, I won't go too far,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.681
but just to throw those out there.

00:10:32.681 --> 00:10:34.877
- Go ahead, peel the
onion layers if you want.

00:10:34.877 --> 00:10:38.430
- I can go off on that for
quite a bit in a good way.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.110
But again, I actually just appreciate you

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.005
asking how I'm doing at
this point in time too.

00:10:43.005 --> 00:10:46.320
So it's refreshing to be
able to have that level

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.153
of conversation.

00:10:47.153 --> 00:10:51.120
I think that candor has
become more commonplace

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:53.340
and I'm hoping that actually sticks

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.140
in some of those circles that we move in.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.453
So definitely something that I appreciate.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:10:59.820
Other thing that I was gonna do,

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:00.960
just to pivot a little bit,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.030
because one of the things
that you're known for,

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:04.890
obviously for those who
are familiar with you

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.590
and for those who aren't
to introduce is mentorship

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:09.630
and something that you spent a lot of time

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:12.030
really investing in as a mentor

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.850
and as a trainer of mentors, so to speak.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:16.350
So I was curious if you'd be able to share

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:18.630
a little bit about how
you got into that work,

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:19.680
and then eventually we'll tie in

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.903
how the mentorship and burnout
piece came about as well.

00:11:22.903 --> 00:11:24.240
- Sure. Yeah.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:25.533
Thanks for asking.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:30.360
Yeah, so my college and
post-college degrees

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.400
are in biology.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:33.870
So specifically I have a PhD

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.720
actually in cell molecular biology,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.580
which means I had lots of opportunities

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:41.370
to be mentored in my
quest to be a scientist.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.250
So I'll right out of the gate say

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:47.100
I had lots of amazing mentors.

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.600
Not everybody was perfect,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.190
and those ended up being incredible

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.050
learning experiences along the way.

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.270
I don't actually think
any mentor can be perfect,

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.357
but I think as a trainee,
sometimes we expect that,

00:11:57.357 --> 00:12:00.057
I'm not sure what
perfection is in mentorship,

00:12:00.057 --> 00:12:03.000
but that's something we can unpack too.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.640
But along the way,
especially in graduate school

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.950
and my post-graduate work,

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.530
I just kept finding
myself more and more drawn

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:14.730
to questions around how
scientists were trained

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.010
to do things outside, in my case,

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:20.430
the research biology research laboratory

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.350
to do the other work
they were called to do

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.900
if they were to go on as faculty

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:26.280
or in any kind of leadership.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.700
And these would include roles in teaching

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:31.680
and in mentoring other folks.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.470
And the ability to teach and mentor

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:38.340
has huge impacts on those
with whom they work,

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:41.340
but very few scientists
are formally trained

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:43.050
how to do either.

00:12:43.050 --> 00:12:47.040
And so that became very
much a point of interest

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.750
and fascination for me.

00:12:48.750 --> 00:12:50.940
And so over the last 18 years,

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.330
I've had this amazing
opportunity to investigate

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.680
in particular mentorship.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.540
I also did a lot of work
in teaching and learning

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.750
but we'll focus on mentorship for today.

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:04.410
And that led to helping develop programs

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:05.580
to enhance mentorship.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:09.660
And that led to developing
interventions or trainings

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.200
to help folks become better
mentors and better mentees.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:16.710
And then that research bug in my head

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:20.284
really kept me talking to evaluators

00:13:20.284 --> 00:13:23.100
and social scientists to understand

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:26.010
how those types of trainings work.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:28.200
And then finding out that there were lots

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:30.480
of research questions we could ask about

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:33.090
what does it mean to be in an effective

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.340
research mentoring relationship?

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:37.890
What does it mean to have
a good working alliance

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:39.570
with someone?

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:42.060
What are the impacts of mentorship

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.360
and for who and in what context?

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:48.510
And so that's been
really the emergent work

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.130
over almost two decades.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.590
And I've had, I'm not trained

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:53.910
to do any of those things

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:55.500
and have really learned on the job

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:58.200
from amazing mentors, really,

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.880
kind of coming full circle.

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:01.800
So I always like to say
that I'm a scientist

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:04.650
who started by studying microorganisms

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:06.928
and I ended up studying people.

00:14:06.928 --> 00:14:08.430
- I think that's really well put.

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:11.580
And I appreciate the way you've
captured all those pieces.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:12.810
Even the fact that you mentioned

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.190
that you weren't trained,

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:15.900
but that you did learn from mentors,

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:17.370
but then you've also now been invested

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.710
in the training itself and
the science of mentorship,

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:20.910
so to speak, and being able to bring

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:22.110
those two together.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:23.730
And I think that's so important.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.590
Just to share a personal story.

00:14:25.590 --> 00:14:27.570
I remember when I first
started my position, again,

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.160
this is within an academic setting,

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.620
but this obviously applies
in lots of other ways.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:32.633
But I do remember I was participating

00:14:32.633 --> 00:14:34.380
in like in a local conference

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:36.840
and doing a session on mentorship,

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:39.270
but some of the other
more seasoned faculty

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:41.220
basically were thinking,
well, you're brand new.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.416
What can you have to
share about mentorship?

00:14:43.416 --> 00:14:45.180
As if it was something that people

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.680
just learned by osmosis.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:47.880
And just because you're more senior,

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:49.020
that means you're a good mentor.

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:51.270
If you're a junior, then you
must not be a good mentor.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.610
So, in some ways, real, and in many ways

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:56.250
just appreciate the emphasis
that you've put on that.

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:57.300
And I think similar to you,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:58.740
even though I was junior at that point,

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:00.270
I had had really strong mentors

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:01.950
and was able to learn from them

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.450
and apply those things so much so

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:04.470
that people can weigh, oh,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:06.630
you actually did have something to say

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.790
and to share with us as if that was

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:09.623
somehow not.

00:15:09.623 --> 00:15:11.760
- How surprising, Right?

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.730
Yeah, I always love when
people are surprised.

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:18.499
Yeah, and I really think
early on in this work,

00:15:18.499 --> 00:15:23.130
when we asked mentors how
they learned how to mentor,

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.110
we basically got two answers.

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:27.900
Either I learned from watching the mentors

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.160
that I thought were effective

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:30.660
and I like to try to do everything

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.880
they modeled or the opposite

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.560
whereas I had horrible mentors

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.530
and I'm trying to do
everything exactly counter

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:39.480
to what I experienced.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.180
And then it was basically trial and error.

00:15:42.180 --> 00:15:45.300
And even though every
mentoring relationship

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.150
is idiosyncratic, we
have to understand people

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:50.460
are individuals and have individual needs

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.460
and come from different
contexts and backgrounds.

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:57.381
There are some core
principles like communication

00:15:57.381 --> 00:16:01.320
that really folks can
all, including ourselves,

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.870
always optimize and always be able

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.450
to enhance their toolbox.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:07.800
- Yeah, I highly agree.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.450
And even as you shared those two stories,

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:11.880
I mean one theme that jumps
out to me from both of those

00:16:11.880 --> 00:16:13.620
is both of those were intentional.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.240
People were either
intentionally trying to be

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.590
like someone or intentionally trying

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:18.330
to not be like someone else,

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:21.420
but had that intentionality
in how they moved forward.

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:22.830
And again, just in my experience,

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.690
and I'm sure in the work
that you've done too,

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:25.890
the fact that you have the training

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.410
the intentionality is
there, seems to be key.

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:30.090
Because again, that question now is asked

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.040
is almost as if

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:33.450
the person assumed that I hadn't thought

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:35.350
about mentorship because I was junior.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.123
- Right, had it reflected on it.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:43.260
One of my dear friends and colleagues

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:46.770
and also amazing scholar in this area,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:51.243
Dr. Angela Byars-Winston,
who's also here at UW Madison,

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:55.140
she always says, and
we also try to echo in

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.810
all of our mentorship education,

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.390
is that it's not enough
to be well-intentioned,

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.670
you have to be intentional.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.380
And I think to add to that,

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:09.210
And be intentional with tools and evidence

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:10.413
in your back pocket.

00:17:11.310 --> 00:17:15.030
You don't come from a place
of not being informed.

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:16.800
And that doesn't mean
you have to necessarily

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:19.139
have read 50 books on mentorship,

00:17:19.139 --> 00:17:21.090
but take a moment to reflect

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:23.070
on why you're doing things,

00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:24.810
ask the people you're working with

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.730
if it's working for them.

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:28.740
Those are parts of being intentional too.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.590
It's not just, I decided
I'm gonna lose weight

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:33.210
this year and I'm gonna focus on it.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.610
It's like, well, why
hasn't that worked before?

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:37.050
And who's gonna help me?

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:40.410
There's a lot of components
to being intentional.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:41.370
- Well, I appreciate you sharing that,

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:42.870
such wisdom in that,

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:46.230
and that such wisdom that
we can definitely apply

00:17:46.230 --> 00:17:47.880
going forward.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:49.410
And again, I mean, you're demonstrating

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:50.700
what you've already said at the beginning

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.650
of just how you've
taken and learned things

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:54.420
from so many different
people, from your colleagues,

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.310
and just incorporate that in
the work that you're doing.

00:17:56.310 --> 00:18:00.160
So I appreciate that you're
practicing what you preach

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:01.983
in a lot of ways.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.280
The other thing I know
that people are eager about

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.857
this episode is this
topic of burnout as well.

00:18:07.857 --> 00:18:09.630
And I guess I'm segueing by saying,

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.640
practicing what you preach,
because for our listeners,

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:13.920
we'll also have another
guest who will be joining,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.450
who is actually a pastor and a leader

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:18.480
who also has talked about
these aspects of burnout,

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.730
but then also mentorship and coaching

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.400
in different roles and those
who are in pastor roles

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.930
and in ministry.

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:28.230
So again, we've talked
about this intentionality

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:29.520
and moving things forward,

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:32.340
but there can also be extremes of that

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.870
and not even extremes,

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:35.280
and especially in service roles

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.680
when you're so invested in other people

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:40.410
that can easily lead
to a place of burnout.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:41.910
So Chris, I was wondering
if you could just share

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:44.400
how the burnout topic even came up

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:45.960
within this realm of mentorship

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.970
and what brought that about?

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:49.770
'Cause I imagine it's something that's,

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.110
not just imagined, I know
it's always been there,

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:54.600
but not always talked about.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:58.710
- Yeah, it's actually how the,

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.390
so we wrote a piece,

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:02.550
my colleagues and I, and I'll mention

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:05.400
who I'm talking about in a minute

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:08.460
for the Harvard Business
Review in last year.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:12.210
And it's a funny story
actually, how it came to be.

00:19:12.210 --> 00:19:16.050
So maybe I'll share a
little bit with listeners,

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:19.060
'cause I also think it's
about how things just happen

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:21.750
but you have to be looking for them.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:26.430
There are four of us worked on this piece

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:27.887
for Harvard Business Review.

00:19:27.887 --> 00:19:32.849
So Ruth Gotian and Chaveso
Cook and Brad Johnson.

00:19:32.849 --> 00:19:36.480
And so Ruth is a chief learning officer

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:37.890
and assistant professor

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:41.730
at Weill Cornell Medicine in New York.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:46.080
And then Chaveso goes by Chevy Cook,

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:48.450
he's actually a US Army Lieutenant Colonel

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.860
and he's at Fort Meade,

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:53.700
but he also runs this nonprofit
called Military Mentors.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:55.590
And then Brad Johnson, he's actually

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:57.540
a professor of psychology

00:19:57.540 --> 00:20:00.150
in the Department of
Leadership, Ethics, and Law

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:01.740
at the US Naval Academy.

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:04.650
And he also has an
position at John Hopkins.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:07.050
These three folks have
been kind of what I'll call

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:10.080
in the science and practice
of mentorship world

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:11.310
for quite a while.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.410
And they've written
different types of things

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.300
and they study different types of things

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:16.980
and as you can imagine,

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:19.890
just from hearing where they work.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:22.230
And we've all known
about each other's work

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.150
and we've all been fans
of each other's work,

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:27.810
but we haven't been in the
same spaces necessarily.

00:20:27.810 --> 00:20:31.020
And over the last about
four or five years,

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:34.140
we'd end up finding
ourselves in the same room,

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.130
but not all together.

00:20:35.130 --> 00:20:37.320
So two of us would be at one thing

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.390
and then two of us would
be at something else.

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:41.520
And every time that would happen,

00:20:41.520 --> 00:20:43.860
the group that was
physically together on Zoom

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.200
or physically together in
person or on Zoom would say,

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.620
oh, we have to write something together.

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:51.150
And each of the different folks

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:52.680
had written with each other.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:57.457
So this happened again
in early spring 2022

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:01.627
is Chevy and I were at a meeting, he said,

00:21:01.627 --> 00:21:03.120
"We have to write something together."

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:04.980
And I said, "Oh, well Ruth has been saying

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:06.540
we have to write something else together."

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:09.030
And then Brad was at this meeting too

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:10.440
and he is like, "Well, I
don't have a lot of time

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:11.730
but we should write something together."

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:15.330
So we finally all got on a Zoom call,

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:17.310
no idea what we were gonna write about.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:20.640
Literally just said we
all admire each other,

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.260
we're all thinking in this space,

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:24.780
we should surely if we
got on a call together

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.160
we could come up with something

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:27.423
we should write together.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.880
And so I like to share that part

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.530
because I think that from the outside

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:33.300
it sometimes looks like everything

00:21:33.300 --> 00:21:36.450
is structured and we
came to a conversation

00:21:36.450 --> 00:21:39.270
and you better show up
with a brilliant idea,

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.860
otherwise why would you show up?

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.409
No, we literally got on a call and said,

00:21:42.409 --> 00:21:44.880
"All we wanna do is do something together.

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.800
We don't know what we should do."

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:50.257
And so we started talking
and everybody said,

00:21:50.257 --> 00:21:53.040
"Oh yeah, it would be great
to write something together.

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:54.090
But I'm too busy.

00:21:54.090 --> 00:21:55.260
I'm kind of burnt out.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:56.220
I'm kind of tired."

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:58.147
You can see where this is going.

00:21:58.147 --> 00:22:01.920
"Oh, wait a minute, maybe
I've been talking to mentors

00:22:01.920 --> 00:22:03.840
and they've been talking
about being burnt out

00:22:03.840 --> 00:22:05.520
and I'm kind of feeling burnt out."

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:07.147
And then Brad Johnson said,

00:22:07.147 --> 00:22:08.760
"I've actually written a topic

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.550
about mentor self-care in 2018

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:13.620
and about how to avoid burnout."

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:16.507
And so really like within
an hour we had said,

00:22:16.507 --> 00:22:18.960
"That's it, we've gotta
write something about

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.060
avoiding mentor burnout."

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:23.790
And we literally just set up a Google Doc

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:25.710
and we started throwing things in it.

00:22:25.710 --> 00:22:27.630
And then we met like two more times

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:29.460
and we did the whole thing in a couple,

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.061
like several weeks.

00:22:32.061 --> 00:22:34.800
And then fortuitously,

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:38.070
it turned out that Brad had a connection

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:39.240
at Harvard Business Review.

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.310
They were looking for an article.

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:44.170
So, you know, it just kind
of ha happens that way

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:46.110
in the networking.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.780
But it really was interesting

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:50.910
because when we finished and we wrote it,

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.650
and we can talk more about
what's in the article

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:53.483
if you want,

00:22:54.390 --> 00:22:56.310
we all said

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:59.880
that we felt really re-energized

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:01.410
after writing it

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:04.830
even though we had put
something new on our plate.

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:05.910
And you and I were joking

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:07.890
before we kind of started the podcast

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:10.380
as like, oh, I hope I didn't
like make you feel burnt out

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.090
because I asked you to do the podcast

00:23:12.090 --> 00:23:15.510
or for you thinking you're
doing one more podcast.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.580
But we realize that there is something

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:23.110
in just thinking about burnout

00:23:24.210 --> 00:23:27.540
and thinking through why
am I feeling that way,

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:32.160
What things energize me,
what's drawing my energy away?

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:34.680
And this article energized us

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:38.160
that actually helped
us feel less burnt out

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:40.320
than when we decided to do it.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:42.960
So that's just a little bit of kind of how

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.580
it actually all played out.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.920
And it was a wonderful like thing to do

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:49.080
with three amazing colleagues

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:51.450
that we had all wanted
to do something together

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:53.160
and then we all felt better.

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.373
- Wow, that's amazing.

00:23:55.373 --> 00:23:57.390
I mean, I appreciate hearing

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:58.679
how you all were attentive

00:23:58.679 --> 00:24:01.710
to what you were experiencing
in that moment too.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.030
I mean, just the fact of naming it

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:04.560
seems like it was so,

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.180
cathartic isn't the
right word, but just...

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:07.927
- Yeah, and then someone would say,

00:24:07.927 --> 00:24:09.810
"This week I don't have any time."

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.280
And someone else would
say, "Well, I'll work,

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:13.080
I'll spend 30 minutes on it this week

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.190
and then I'll hand it off."

00:24:14.190 --> 00:24:17.580
And so it became kind of the process

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.619
of like all the things we were saying

00:24:19.619 --> 00:24:23.100
in the article of then saying,
"Wait, am I doing that?"

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:24.540
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.210
- Which is always a little bit scary.

00:24:27.210 --> 00:24:31.260
I thought about when you
asked me to be a guest is

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:33.330
that the thing I was most nervous about

00:24:33.330 --> 00:24:36.210
was not how many ums and not would I say

00:24:36.210 --> 00:24:38.880
something that didn't sound smart,

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:42.720
or it was mostly if my

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:45.600
friends or colleagues or family listen it,

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:48.750
would they tell me that
I had been authentic

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:51.810
to how they were experiencing
me dealing burnout.

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:52.643
- Wow.

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:55.320
Yeah, that makes a lot of sense.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:58.770
I guess it's that level in
a sense of accountability.

00:24:58.770 --> 00:24:59.603
- Yeah, right.

00:24:59.603 --> 00:25:00.436
Was I gonna get on and be like,

00:25:00.436 --> 00:25:02.160
"Well here's how you avoid it."

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:02.993
And then they'd be like,

00:25:02.993 --> 00:25:06.687
"Literally three weeks ago
you were so burnt out."

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:09.000
Just to be honest.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:11.880
And I think that taking
time to just be honest

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:15.390
about what it means when we feel burnt out

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.730
has been really important.

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.860
I was thinking a lot about

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.200
since writing the article
and thinking about it,

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:26.160
I've done a lot better
job of querying burnout.

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:28.860
What does that mean,
when I say I'm burnt out,

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:31.590
or I hear someone say they're burnt out,

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:34.200
first I wanna validate
that in myself and others,

00:25:34.200 --> 00:25:37.320
but also to say what
does that actually mean?

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:40.110
Does it mean I'm exhausted to the point

00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:42.510
that I need to go sleep for three days?

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.330
Does it mean I'm actually
just irritated by everybody

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:46.380
asking me for something

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:49.623
and actually if I take a
walk, I'm gonna be okay?

00:25:50.580 --> 00:25:53.580
Or am I really at a place of such burnout

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:55.680
that I'm being cynical,

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:57.960
I'm being inefficient in everything I do

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.929
'cause my mind's wandering
cause I can't focus.

00:26:00.929 --> 00:26:04.680
But that's been really helpful

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:07.890
in listening for in myself and others

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:10.890
is when we say that just investigating it

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:11.723
a little bit.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:13.553
- That's really good.

00:26:13.553 --> 00:26:15.030
How have you helped people to do that?

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:15.863
I'm curious and even in a way

00:26:15.863 --> 00:26:18.240
'cause I know you centered the article

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:20.010
kind of in the history of the term,

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:21.885
so I'm curious how you have

00:26:21.885 --> 00:26:23.779
suggested that people apply that

00:26:23.779 --> 00:26:25.680
to their own situations,

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.750
and then also based on
how they apply that,

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:29.190
how they actually move forward

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:32.130
to kind of move out of that
place of those different,

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:34.440
not tiers, but different modes of burnout.

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:35.670
- Yeah, and I'm certainly

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.679
not an expert in helping people.

00:26:37.679 --> 00:26:39.060
Definitely not a therapist,

00:26:39.060 --> 00:26:42.243
although my mother is one.

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:47.100
But I do think that, well
I could even say to you,

00:26:47.100 --> 00:26:50.970
so the last time you felt really burnt out

00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:55.860
is think about what you were feeling,

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.633
think about the weight of it.

00:26:58.775 --> 00:27:00.870
How big did it feel?

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.570
Did it feel all consuming?

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:06.300
Was it only in certain contexts?

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:07.890
Was it every day?

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:10.540
Was it only when you were
dealing with certain issues

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:14.850
to try like to just
examine it a little bit.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:19.230
So I think that it's not one specific way,

00:27:19.230 --> 00:27:21.800
but to allow someone to explore,

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:24.420
tell me what that feels like,

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:26.790
tell me when that happens,

00:27:26.790 --> 00:27:30.420
tell me about what you do
when you feel like that.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:34.410
So that then we can kind of say, oh wow.

00:27:34.410 --> 00:27:38.310
So it's sounding like
whenever you think about,

00:27:38.310 --> 00:27:40.320
let's just use for example

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.390
the enormous weight of parental care

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:43.950
that you're engaged in,

00:27:43.950 --> 00:27:48.060
that's where it's really feeling enormous

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:51.750
and that it's trickling
into all of your work,

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:53.220
but that is at its core

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:55.200
is where your burnout's coming from.

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:58.683
And so what could you do around that?

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:01.680
- Yeah, I think that's really helpful.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:04.020
And I mean, even just
doing a mental exercise

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:05.610
as you were asking that and thinking about

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:07.440
getting back to even mentoring
all the different types

00:28:07.440 --> 00:28:09.180
of mentoring roles that I have.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:11.160
And if I had to say, oh
well I couldn't notice

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:13.290
some specific patterns
of certain types of roles

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:14.640
where there was more of a burnout feel

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:17.730
or feeling too spread
thin and thinking about,

00:28:17.730 --> 00:28:19.923
well then I might not feel
like I have as much time

00:28:19.923 --> 00:28:21.240
to respond to those things

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:22.800
or feeling more irritable
about those things.

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:23.850
So even in that exercise,

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.370
I think that's really helpful
to kind of piece through it

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:28.533
and think about in that
very pragmatic way.

00:28:29.692 --> 00:28:31.440
And I'm also curious, as you wrote,

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.630
how do people respond to the article?

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:36.720
Because I could see it
going in different ways.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.460
I could see it being
refreshing for people.

00:28:38.460 --> 00:28:41.403
I could see sometimes
people being reactive to it,

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:43.950
maybe not maybe needing to hear it,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:45.237
but not wanting to,

00:28:45.237 --> 00:28:46.500
and not to put words in your mouth,

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.750
but those are just things
that kind of come to mind

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:49.680
as I think about people,

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:51.150
how people might have reacted.

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:52.887
But I'm curious what your experience was

00:28:52.887 --> 00:28:54.630
and how people responded.

00:28:54.630 --> 00:28:58.110
- Yeah, I think you hit
the nail on the head

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:01.020
is it's a little bit of everything.

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:04.120
So I think for some it

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:06.540
provided validation

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:07.800
for how they were feeling,

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:09.240
that they could say, yeah,

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:12.900
I know this mentoring work is important,

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:15.183
but it's exhausting sometimes.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:18.063
And just to be able to say it,

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.510
it doesn't mean you're not
committed to mentoring,

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:23.580
it doesn't mean you don't want a good job,

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:26.007
but just it's okay to
say that it takes energy

00:29:26.007 --> 00:29:27.153
and and you're tired.

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.150
I think it encouraged some mentors

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:32.100
to take a minute to look
for signs of burnout

00:29:32.100 --> 00:29:33.810
in themselves and others and be honest

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:34.643
about where they are,

00:29:34.643 --> 00:29:37.950
but then the answer could be difficult.

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:41.846
And I think when the
answer is, I am burnt out

00:29:41.846 --> 00:29:46.846
and I feel like I need to
take a break from mentoring

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:49.740
and I feel like I'm in
a position that I can't

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:51.990
because of who, the consequences,

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.430
whether that be political,

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.950
whether that be for promotion and tenure

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.470
in the academic setting,
whether that be consequential

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:00.020
to the people you're mentoring.

00:30:01.080 --> 00:30:04.470
But one of the things we tried
to raise in the article is,

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:06.210
was kind of we didn't call it this,

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:08.850
but it was kinda like if you're faking it.

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:10.980
So if you're trying to mentor from a place

00:30:10.980 --> 00:30:12.870
of exhaustion and burnout,

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:16.740
you're probably not doing a good job.

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:19.980
And this was actually couched in a term

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:23.643
by one of the wonderful
mentorship scholars,

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:25.740
Belle Rose Ragins,

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:28.830
who actually called it marginal mentoring,

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:31.410
where you're basically
engaged in dysfunctional

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:35.460
or disengaged mentoring
because you're so depleted.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:37.020
So you might think, fine,

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:39.180
I'll meet with all seven of my mentees.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:40.560
But if you're really doing that

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:43.290
from a place of exhaustion,

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:45.300
there's consequences to that

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.270
in terms of the kind
of mentorship you are.

00:30:48.270 --> 00:30:49.870
And I think that was the other thing

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:53.130
that we really got some
interesting feedback on

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:56.010
was the permission and suggestion

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:58.443
to talk about burnout with your mentees,

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:02.700
to normalize that conversation

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:05.010
that there are imperfections,

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:07.710
if you don't wanna call
it, there's normalcy,

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:10.080
there's coping, there's avoiding.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:15.060
And that when mentors
continue to try to model

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:18.153
that they never get burnt
out and they're never tired,

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:24.243
the mentees begin to believe
they cannot do that job.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:26.370
Because they'll say,

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:27.600
I'll never be able to do that

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:29.733
without saying I'm tired sometimes.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:34.860
So I think that was one piece
we wanted to give people

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:39.780
and the encouragement to be
realistic and honest about,

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:41.430
there's ups and downs, there's burnout,

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:44.430
there's exhaustion,
and model how to do it.

00:31:44.430 --> 00:31:46.530
Model as a mentor of saying,

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:48.960
I can't meet with you this week.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:50.820
It's not because I don't care,

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:53.010
it's because I actually
probably won't be helpful

00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:54.693
'cause I'm just burnt out.

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:57.903
And that being okay.

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:01.020
I mean obviously there's content,

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:02.213
people can be in crisis.

00:32:02.213 --> 00:32:04.800
We're not saying like
someone's in crisis and saying,

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:07.260
I can't help you, at least to
refer them to someone else,

00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:08.853
but in the day-to-day.

00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:12.630
And I think back to that there's risk

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:14.463
of just pushing through as a mentor.

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:19.200
So I think those were some of the things

00:32:19.200 --> 00:32:21.060
in terms of feedback.

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.020
And I think you're right.

00:32:22.020 --> 00:32:25.230
I mean for some folks we
don't wanna think about it.

00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.600
or mentors say, well yeah that's nice,

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.040
but I'm trapped.

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:30.513
I'm not a big,

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.160
I remember all the way back to high school

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:40.320
is one of my most influential teachers,

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.000
one of the exercises they would do

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:43.650
is that whenever you were feeling trapped,

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:44.850
they said you had to come up

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:46.680
with at least three approaches

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:48.750
to the situation you were in.

00:32:48.750 --> 00:32:51.270
You could not only come up with two.

00:32:51.270 --> 00:32:52.890
And I always think back to that

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:55.980
because I think when we feel trapped,

00:32:55.980 --> 00:32:59.070
the exercise of saying how could I get out

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:00.600
or how could I change it,

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:05.190
is to try to think beyond
one or two options.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:07.350
Now that's also contextual.

00:33:07.350 --> 00:33:10.680
So I know one of the things
that we can talk about

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.960
in a little bit is not
everybody gets asked

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:16.650
the same amount of service
work or mentorship.

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:21.650
Not everybody has the
ability to have folks help.

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:25.260
Some folks have a lot of
additional family help,

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:27.870
they have access to money
that they can throw out

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:28.863
the problem.

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:31.032
Folks will be like, you're burnt out.

00:33:31.032 --> 00:33:33.390
Just go on a spa vacation.

00:33:33.390 --> 00:33:36.000
Well that's not accessible
to a lot of people

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:37.170
for a lot of reasons.

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:39.930
And that's lovely if you can do that,

00:33:39.930 --> 00:33:41.836
but that's probably not an answer

00:33:41.836 --> 00:33:44.790
that's gonna really resonate
with a lot of people

00:33:44.790 --> 00:33:47.610
who feel like that's
completely inconsiderate

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:50.913
of inequity and access
to those kind of things.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:55.050
Taking a walk, it's a
little bit different.

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:57.180
Believing you don't have
five minutes to walk

00:33:57.180 --> 00:33:59.850
because you feel so crunched

00:33:59.850 --> 00:34:01.170
and saying to somebody,

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:03.390
I really wanna encourage
you to just take a moment

00:34:03.390 --> 00:34:05.100
and hear what you're saying,

00:34:05.100 --> 00:34:06.810
that you don't have five minutes.

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:09.420
That makes me sad that you feel that way

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:12.163
so let's talk about why you feel that way.

00:34:12.163 --> 00:34:14.489
But yeah, so I think the,

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:18.101
an article like this
triggers all of those things.

00:34:18.101 --> 00:34:19.440
- I mean there's so many layers there

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:21.300
and just the way that you've
brought all those to light,

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:23.190
even as you were talking
about the modeling

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:25.870
as we talked about from
the beginning, mentoring

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:29.310
well from learning from either
good models or poor models

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:31.470
and just the way that
acknowledging that burnout

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:34.260
can be such an example I think is huge.

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:36.210
But then even all the other
pieces that you mentioned

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.190
as well and a lot to unpack there,

00:34:38.190 --> 00:34:41.100
but just the guilt that
comes with that sometimes,

00:34:41.100 --> 00:34:43.500
the perfectionism that
come with that sometimes.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:45.423
But then even the
inequities as you mentioned

00:34:45.423 --> 00:34:48.240
and all the complexity
that comes with that

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:49.073
topic that I'm sure,

00:34:49.073 --> 00:34:50.700
and I know you all touched on as well,

00:34:50.700 --> 00:34:54.750
is the mentor as well and
whether it's a female mentor,

00:34:54.750 --> 00:34:56.640
whether it's someone from
an underrepresented group,

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:58.800
and the additional burden that comes

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.200
just in conversations that
I've had with so many people

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.960
in personal experience of knowing that

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:05.460
some of our mentees feel like

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:07.110
they can't go to anybody else,

00:35:07.110 --> 00:35:10.530
which then adds another layer of guilt

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:11.910
in the sense that we can think

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:14.160
if we don't mentor this person, who will?

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:16.350
And there is truth to
that to some extent, too,

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:20.790
but then how much that can
also lead towards that burnout.

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:22.530
So I appreciate the way
that you brought that up

00:35:22.530 --> 00:35:23.363
in the article.

00:35:23.363 --> 00:35:24.810
And I'm just curious

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:26.520
if there's anything you'd
want to expand on that,

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:29.070
on that tension, that real
tension that's there as well?

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.350
Because obviously you
can also get to a point,

00:35:31.350 --> 00:35:32.970
as you mentioned, of mentoring so many

00:35:32.970 --> 00:35:34.560
that you become ineffective.

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:35.820
So I know there's no perfect,

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:38.669
there is no perfect
answer, but how have you,

00:35:38.669 --> 00:35:40.080
have you all at least described that

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:41.910
and how have you been thinking
about some of those...

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:43.320
- Yeah, and I don't know that,

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.353
when you're writing a short article for,

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:52.080
for a publication like
Harv Business Review,

00:35:52.080 --> 00:35:53.190
you're gonna get crunched

00:35:53.190 --> 00:35:55.800
and so you can't really
unpack all of that.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:58.556
You can leave little
breadcrumb trails for that

00:35:58.556 --> 00:36:02.730
but I think that's one
of the great things is

00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:04.860
you get a lot of people
kind of thinking about it,

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:08.610
a lot of publicity around it,

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:11.130
but you don't get to
really dig into nuance.

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:15.750
And that can rub people
the wrong way, absolutely.

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:18.540
Because you get that sense
like it's not that simple.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:21.420
And then part of it is

00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:23.850
hoping that the authors
know it's not that simple

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:25.953
and we're limited by space.

00:36:26.880 --> 00:36:30.750
But also there's sometimes
when authors miss things.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:32.220
I'm sure we missed things too,

00:36:32.220 --> 00:36:34.860
or folks thought, did you not consider?

00:36:34.860 --> 00:36:37.650
And there are probably
many thoughts people

00:36:37.650 --> 00:36:39.870
had about things that we didn't consider

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:41.880
and is room to grow.

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:44.730
I do think you raise, especially around,

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:46.713
I mean we know, you know,

00:36:47.572 --> 00:36:51.720
and I'm sure many of the
guests you've had before know

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:55.140
that individuals from
underrepresented groups,

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:57.030
they get asked to do more of this work.

00:36:57.030 --> 00:37:00.180
I mean, let's just more
of the mentorship work.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.640
When we're talking
about academic settings,

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.070
we know they're asked to
do more mentorship work,

00:37:05.070 --> 00:37:08.670
more equity inclusion,
diversity, justice work,

00:37:08.670 --> 00:37:11.520
more psychosocial work.

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:14.400
So there's a huge inequity
about what they're asked to do.

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:18.210
And it's not just what they're
asked to do by leadership,

00:37:18.210 --> 00:37:20.470
it's what they're sometimes

00:37:21.330 --> 00:37:23.520
articulated and not articulated

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:25.563
expected to do by their peers.

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:29.880
And then what they are asked to do

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:32.970
by folks who feel like they share

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:35.430
certain identities with them.

00:37:35.430 --> 00:37:39.570
And even though I think
there is a real call

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.580
to white identified folks like myself

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:43.800
to take some of that burden on

00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:45.870
and to do the work that's needed

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:47.820
to be more culturally responsive

00:37:47.820 --> 00:37:49.830
and to be a more culturally
responsive mentor,

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:52.560
and we do a lot of that type of training,

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:54.000
is at the end of the day,

00:37:54.000 --> 00:37:57.060
is there's a lot of mentees that are wary,

00:37:57.060 --> 00:38:00.540
that don't trust, have
every right not to trust

00:38:00.540 --> 00:38:02.760
that someone like me could hear them

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:07.709
and support them no matter
how much I've prepped.

00:38:07.709 --> 00:38:10.950
And at a minimum, even if they believe it,

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:12.794
don't wanna start there.

00:38:12.794 --> 00:38:15.720
And so that puts additional burden

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:17.250
on folks like yourself.

00:38:17.250 --> 00:38:21.090
And so I know there's a
lot of talk and effort

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:24.870
to make that work valued
and to compensate it,

00:38:24.870 --> 00:38:25.893
but again,

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:29.643
no amount of compensation buys you time.

00:38:32.010 --> 00:38:34.000
It doesn't buy you

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:38.013
a refill on the emotional drain.

00:38:39.394 --> 00:38:42.720
And so I think it should still be done.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:46.320
I'm a big fan of being
compensated for that work.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:48.450
And it can't be the only answer

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:52.590
because it can't solve all of the burnout

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:57.063
that is unequally divided amongst people.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.760
- Yeah, that's really well said.

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:01.230
And even as you were talking about it

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:03.990
really resonate with the
not being able to buy time.

00:39:03.990 --> 00:39:05.880
But in a way it also made me think about

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:08.640
the experience that you
all had as authors as well,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:10.740
even just naming that burnout

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:12.550
and just the power that can also come

00:39:12.550 --> 00:39:14.760
from having conversations like this,

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:17.910
or for those of us who are
from overlapping backgrounds,

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:20.400
having the space to talk
about that together.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.620
And again, this is just
anecdotal, this is not

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:24.600
evidence-based, but just
as I've been reflecting

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:25.433
on some of the things that you said

00:39:25.433 --> 00:39:27.540
and so many other guests have shared

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:30.090
about the work that they
do, just being in the room,

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:31.710
I mean so many people
have come on this podcast

00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:33.180
and even made that type of comment

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:35.250
about the conversations they've had here.

00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:37.080
Not that it removes the challenges

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:38.100
or the burnout that may come,

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:39.690
but just to be able to
kind of walk through that

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.220
and think collectively about ways

00:39:41.220 --> 00:39:44.230
to navigate that I think can
be really powerful as well.

00:39:44.230 --> 00:39:45.720
- Well and it could be compounded.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.763
we know that one of the things,

00:39:49.950 --> 00:39:51.570
that I think one we talked about

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:52.770
when we were working on the article

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:55.650
was data like women physicians

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:57.720
spend on average two minutes more

00:39:57.720 --> 00:40:01.170
on each patient than men

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:03.390
and are more likely to
explore the socio-emotional

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:05.160
and psychosocial issues during the visit.

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:08.240
And I know Brad Johnson
had referred to that

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:10.080
in some of his earlier work.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:11.670
So it feels like just two minutes,

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:16.530
but two minutes and the type
of emotional work over time,

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:20.340
across a day, across a week, across a year

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:24.183
It takes a toll and it's not equal.

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.460
I think the other thing
about burnout too is

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:30.093
I have a lot of,

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:33.210
feel very honored that
I have a lot of friends

00:40:33.210 --> 00:40:37.500
and colleagues who will
challenge me, call me out.

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:40.020
And I remember one colleague saying,

00:40:40.020 --> 00:40:42.390
this was way before we
worked on the article,

00:40:42.390 --> 00:40:44.310
but I remember saying something like,

00:40:44.310 --> 00:40:48.060
oh, I'm just feeling so
burnt out on all this work.

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:51.450
And she knew I acknowledged
there was inequity

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:53.189
and who held work and all of that,

00:40:53.189 --> 00:40:56.160
but it was this kind of
off the cuff comment,

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:57.087
but it has sat with me

00:40:57.087 --> 00:40:59.253
and I think there's so much power in it.

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:04.170
And she is a Black identified
individual and she said,

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.730
I've felt burnt out.

00:41:05.730 --> 00:41:07.140
You feel burnout right now,

00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:10.020
I feel burnout every week of every day

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:11.673
for my entire career.

00:41:12.750 --> 00:41:16.053
And I just had to stop.

00:41:17.220 --> 00:41:22.220
And even think about folks
talking about burnout,

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:24.543
wanting to honor their burnout,

00:41:25.500 --> 00:41:28.983
but also calling people to
contextualize their burnout.

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:32.670
Even burnout has privilege

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:35.520
in that we've had episodic burnout

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:37.800
as opposed to consistent burnout.

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:39.480
And I think those are just things, again,

00:41:39.480 --> 00:41:42.040
I wanna honor the individual
experience and not

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:45.330
devalue anyone saying they feel burnout.

00:41:45.330 --> 00:41:46.260
That's not my intent.

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:49.110
But I think it's important
for us to realize

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:51.360
that even in our speaking into the space,

00:41:51.360 --> 00:41:55.290
we are not all talking about
the same level of burnout,

00:41:55.290 --> 00:41:57.360
the same causes of burnout

00:41:57.360 --> 00:41:59.510
or the frequency at
which we're feeling it.

00:42:00.480 --> 00:42:01.890
- Yeah.

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:03.537
I think that's really well said as well.

00:42:03.537 --> 00:42:04.370
And just, again.

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:06.150
- I have no idea what it would feel like

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:08.520
to feel burnt out my entire career

00:42:08.520 --> 00:42:11.310
every single month of my career.

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:14.970
That is an absolute foreign concept to me

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:17.213
'cause I've had the
privilege not to feel it.

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:22.980
- But even in hearing
that challenging story,

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:25.137
part of me feels
encouraged that you all had

00:42:25.137 --> 00:42:28.530
the space to have that conversation.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:29.460
And then as you've mentioned,

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:30.840
even with the article itself too,

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:32.880
like there's just such a need

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:34.650
to be able to unpack that.

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:36.068
I think like in that context,

00:42:36.068 --> 00:42:38.220
even if it's, let's say there were two

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:40.110
other individuals who are
having that same conversation

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:41.910
to be able to then delve in

00:42:41.910 --> 00:42:44.850
and really understand what that means.

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.310
So much learning that
can happen from that.

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:48.750
And the learning obviously isn't enough

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:49.920
in and of itself,

00:42:49.920 --> 00:42:51.570
but again, it just seems like it creates

00:42:51.570 --> 00:42:53.310
this opportunity in a sense.

00:42:53.310 --> 00:42:55.500
And maybe not being too optimistic,

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:57.990
but just to have community in that sense

00:42:57.990 --> 00:42:59.820
to be able to share those
two different experiences,

00:42:59.820 --> 00:43:00.930
even you saying, I can't imagine

00:43:00.930 --> 00:43:02.280
what that would feel like to have someone

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:05.040
voice that to you and
then to have a dialogue.

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:07.823
I think there's a lot of power
that comes from that as well.

00:43:08.730 --> 00:43:09.563
- Yeah.

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:16.260
It can be such a scary space

00:43:16.260 --> 00:43:19.533
and it's where the most
amazing learning is happening.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:23.790
Which is why I'm so
appreciative you're doing

00:43:23.790 --> 00:43:26.703
the podcast series because
it creates space for that.

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:30.660
I was saying before we started,

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:33.750
I was really so loving
the experiment in you

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:36.060
being accommodating of the schedules

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:37.710
of myself and Dr. Ireland.

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:39.824
And I would've loved to
been on the same space,

00:43:39.824 --> 00:43:43.830
but the fact you had put us together

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:46.090
got me really thinking

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:49.683
about my faith,

00:43:50.550 --> 00:43:52.890
my Christian upbringing,

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:57.287
what's the narrative in my
head and heart about service

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:04.010
and being a servant and
being selfless and burnout.

00:44:04.500 --> 00:44:06.750
And so I'm really excited to hear

00:44:06.750 --> 00:44:09.060
the whole podcast world together,

00:44:09.060 --> 00:44:11.416
to hear thoughts on that.

00:44:11.416 --> 00:44:14.530
But I really, I've really spent

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.270
a couple weeks, not consistently,

00:44:18.270 --> 00:44:22.810
but since your invitation wrestling around

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:29.150
the messaging and my
belief that we are called,

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:32.610
in my Christian faith and in other faiths,

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:36.033
but I can only speak from my lived faith,

00:44:39.231 --> 00:44:42.607
to embrace service to the
point of selflessness.

00:44:44.223 --> 00:44:45.630
And that we're,

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:46.950
the narrative that's written

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:49.170
is that that will fulfill you.

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:51.873
That you will gain energy from that.

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:55.920
But then I know that if you give

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:58.203
to the point of collapse,
it serves no one.

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:04.580
And so just sitting in that paradox,

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:07.350
for lack of a better word,

00:45:07.350 --> 00:45:09.547
I don't have an answer, but I've realized

00:45:09.547 --> 00:45:13.110
I haven't reconciled those

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:15.093
and this you brought up the word guilt,

00:45:17.587 --> 00:45:19.620
and I've been of late,

00:45:19.620 --> 00:45:22.200
been working with a really
wonderful leadership coach

00:45:22.200 --> 00:45:25.140
in the last year and I,

00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:26.610
you're probably not surprised,

00:45:26.610 --> 00:45:29.430
I totally subscribe to servant leadership.

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:31.650
But as our work has grown,

00:45:31.650 --> 00:45:35.640
I have realized that that
can't be my only approach

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:37.830
because it's actually impossible

00:45:37.830 --> 00:45:41.490
'cause I can't be in service to everyone

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:42.990
and just keep it growing.

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:44.670
There becomes a limit.

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:48.990
And so I have, I'm having to
change my leadership style

00:45:48.990 --> 00:45:50.790
and I feel a little guilty about it.

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:52.950
So yeah, you're saying yes,

00:45:52.950 --> 00:45:54.330
like you've been, so I'd love

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:56.700
to hear your thoughts on this too and,

00:45:56.700 --> 00:45:59.010
but I hadn't considered how wrapped up

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:02.040
that struggle was in my faith

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:04.530
and this has really
afforded me the opportunity

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:08.010
and to really think about that

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:11.520
because of the pairing you
put together for the podcast.

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:14.070
I don't think I would've
gone there in my thinking.

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:16.560
But I'm curious like, yeah, you had

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:18.270
a very interesting look on your face

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:20.430
so I'm really curious what
you're thinking right now.

00:46:20.430 --> 00:46:22.440
- Yeah, you definitely picked up on it.

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:24.840
And partially, I mean, before we wrap up,

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:25.860
I'm gonna also ask you

