00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:41.540
is so much empathy for that stage of life.

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:44.766
It is challenging.

00:58:44.766 --> 00:58:46.890
- Yeah, and I had forgotten that

00:58:46.890 --> 00:58:48.750
until I had been around family members

00:58:48.750 --> 00:58:51.270
with young kids again
and it took me back then,

00:58:51.270 --> 00:58:53.120
I didn't know how I'd forgotten that.

00:58:54.900 --> 00:58:56.694
Well again, just really appreciate,

00:58:56.694 --> 00:59:00.420
just your honesty with so
many different aspects.

00:59:00.420 --> 00:59:01.323
I'm gonna venture a guess to say

00:59:01.323 --> 00:59:03.626
that this is not a typical conversation

00:59:03.626 --> 00:59:06.030
that you have on a daily basis

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:09.420
in your work in terms of the
things that we've spanned.

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:10.950
But I really appreciate.

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:12.600
You want us to be able
to kind of think through

00:59:12.600 --> 00:59:13.890
and wrestle with those two.

00:59:13.890 --> 00:59:16.380
And even as you've brought
up the faith aspect,

00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:18.269
and that's something that's
definitely near and dear

00:59:18.269 --> 00:59:20.553
to me and to what we try
to do on this podcast

00:59:20.553 --> 00:59:22.920
just to have deep, honest conversations

00:59:22.920 --> 00:59:24.300
about all aspects of our lives

00:59:24.300 --> 00:59:26.730
and not get stuck in this
kind of compartmentalizing

00:59:26.730 --> 00:59:29.460
and pretending that there
are aspects of our lives

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:30.750
that exist, whether we come from

00:59:30.750 --> 00:59:32.310
a faith perspective or not.

00:59:32.310 --> 00:59:34.530
So I really appreciate your willingness

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:36.330
and we'll definitely pass
some of those questions

00:59:36.330 --> 00:59:37.770
along as well.

00:59:37.770 --> 00:59:39.930
One last question I did
want to ask you though

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:40.920
comes from our listeners

00:59:40.920 --> 00:59:42.180
and particularly because people

00:59:42.180 --> 00:59:45.840
were so excited about
this topic of burnout.

00:59:45.840 --> 00:59:47.490
So one of our listeners asked how

00:59:48.450 --> 00:59:50.610
to avoid or at least deal with burnout,

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:53.010
and this is specifically
within academic context,

00:59:53.010 --> 00:59:55.200
but it expands elsewhere as well.

00:59:55.200 --> 00:59:58.581
But just as people kind
of get more and more roles

00:59:58.581 --> 01:00:01.560
and responsibilities
with career development

01:00:01.560 --> 01:00:03.270
and achievement and things like that,

01:00:03.270 --> 01:00:05.310
and I won't call it the prom,

01:00:05.310 --> 01:00:07.230
but the challenge of people who seem

01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:09.750
to be doing things well
and then seem to get more

01:00:09.750 --> 01:00:12.090
and more added onto their plate.

01:00:12.090 --> 01:00:13.783
And just knowing how to navigate that.

01:00:13.783 --> 01:00:16.290
I don't know if you
have any specific words

01:00:16.290 --> 01:00:18.480
of wisdom you wanna share there.

01:00:18.480 --> 01:00:20.580
- Yeah, so I mean I do,

01:00:20.580 --> 01:00:22.230
I think I'm gonna come back to

01:00:22.230 --> 01:00:26.340
kind of what I started
to share in the journey

01:00:26.340 --> 01:00:27.690
of even writing the piece.

01:00:27.690 --> 01:00:32.690
And then what I've noticed
in my own practice is,

01:00:33.210 --> 01:00:34.410
and it might sound simple,

01:00:34.410 --> 01:00:39.410
but is really, and I'll use
the word querying burnout.

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:46.623
I was listening to and I,

01:00:47.820 --> 01:00:49.210
embarrassed to say I don't remember

01:00:49.210 --> 01:00:51.240
what podcast it was.

01:00:51.240 --> 01:00:54.360
But don't worry, I will never
forget that it's this podcast.

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:57.000
But I realized the irony
in me saying that as I,

01:00:57.000 --> 01:00:57.840
I listened to something

01:00:57.840 --> 01:01:00.330
and I didn't remember whose it was.

01:01:00.330 --> 01:01:02.970
So sorry to that person, but
it was really interesting.

01:01:02.970 --> 01:01:07.970
It was about someone who
had a very, very high,

01:01:09.330 --> 01:01:10.300
high pressure job

01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:14.490
leadership position and
just felt so burnt out

01:01:14.490 --> 01:01:16.053
that they just left.

01:01:17.190 --> 01:01:18.540
That's it.

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:23.540
And then in this person's
story, they went on,

01:01:23.790 --> 01:01:25.260
they actually, I think they went and lived

01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:26.883
on an island actually.

01:01:27.780 --> 01:01:29.520
if I'm remembering it right.

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:31.710
And poor person, if they've
ever listened to this,

01:01:31.710 --> 01:01:33.930
is gonna think I'm completely
slaughtering their story

01:01:33.930 --> 01:01:35.100
so I apologize.

01:01:35.100 --> 01:01:38.190
but the basic idea was
completely burnt out,

01:01:38.190 --> 01:01:39.450
decided the only answer

01:01:39.450 --> 01:01:41.403
was to completely remove themselves.

01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:45.520
Went and basically did nothing

01:01:46.368 --> 01:01:49.620
and had the opportunity to do that,

01:01:49.620 --> 01:01:51.570
most of us can't do that,

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:55.323
but then missed everything
about their work.

01:01:56.618 --> 01:01:58.590
And I only raised it because

01:01:58.590 --> 01:02:00.690
some of the reflections
then that came back,

01:02:00.690 --> 01:02:02.790
they did then end up
being going back to work

01:02:02.790 --> 01:02:07.790
and was that it wasn't all
the things on their list,

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:09.690
it was perspective.

01:02:09.690 --> 01:02:13.083
They actually loved most
of the work they did.

01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:17.580
It actually energized
them a lot of the time.

01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:20.730
They liked their colleagues
and working with them.

01:02:20.730 --> 01:02:24.123
They were passionate about
doing something meaningful.

01:02:26.550 --> 01:02:29.400
But it had all gotten a bit much,

01:02:29.400 --> 01:02:33.330
they couldn't prioritize,
they couldn't focus.

01:02:33.330 --> 01:02:34.740
It was all just too much.

01:02:34.740 --> 01:02:39.570
And the inability to
have perspective on it

01:02:39.570 --> 01:02:42.450
just seemed like the
only thing you could do

01:02:42.450 --> 01:02:43.500
was to just lead.

01:02:43.500 --> 01:02:45.180
And I think we've all been there,

01:02:45.180 --> 01:02:48.510
when we look at everything we
feel we're being asked to do

01:02:48.510 --> 01:02:49.860
or we even feel compelled to do

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:51.453
and we go, it's impossible.

01:02:52.320 --> 01:02:55.200
And I think that the querying of it

01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:57.930
in the burnout is saying, okay,

01:02:57.930 --> 01:02:59.733
it is probably impossible.

01:03:00.600 --> 01:03:03.813
So I could go the extreme
and do none of it,

01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:07.230
that has consequences,

01:03:07.230 --> 01:03:11.610
or I can think about which
pieces I'm gonna do or not.

01:03:11.610 --> 01:03:14.280
And then obviously, like
you said in community

01:03:14.280 --> 01:03:17.820
start to query that and what is draining?

01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:19.560
What is sustaining?

01:03:19.560 --> 01:03:21.183
How are those balanced?

01:03:22.230 --> 01:03:24.463
And so I really do think, especially

01:03:24.463 --> 01:03:27.240
if we're talking about junior colleagues

01:03:27.240 --> 01:03:30.870
in academia who see this

01:03:30.870 --> 01:03:32.670
place that they have to get to

01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:37.110
in order to be promoted
and it feels so high impact

01:03:37.110 --> 01:03:38.673
and so high stakes.

01:03:40.230 --> 01:03:43.020
And then all of the other
things are being asked to do

01:03:43.020 --> 01:03:46.530
and compelled to do and starting to query

01:03:46.530 --> 01:03:49.020
about what sustains, what burns out,

01:03:49.020 --> 01:03:51.153
what's gonna pay out, what's not,

01:03:51.990 --> 01:03:54.390
and to not do that alone.

01:03:54.390 --> 01:03:58.290
I think when we do that alone,

01:03:58.290 --> 01:04:03.180
everything about our own
biases or all our habits,

01:04:03.180 --> 01:04:05.100
they all play out.

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:06.930
And sometimes like we, it's interesting,

01:04:06.930 --> 01:04:09.030
we both talk about coaches,

01:04:09.030 --> 01:04:11.160
someone reflecting back,

01:04:11.160 --> 01:04:12.480
and you've probably had
this experience back,

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:16.710
if they reflected back
and you go, oh, right.

01:04:16.710 --> 01:04:18.960
So actually what you've heard me saying

01:04:18.960 --> 01:04:21.570
is there's really one major priority

01:04:21.570 --> 01:04:23.040
that I wanna get done this month

01:04:23.040 --> 01:04:25.230
and I should spend time on that.

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:28.290
And then suddenly it's clear,

01:04:28.290 --> 01:04:30.360
but I couldn't see it myself.

01:04:30.360 --> 01:04:33.150
So that may be to your listeners

01:04:33.150 --> 01:04:34.650
feel like way too straightforward,

01:04:34.650 --> 01:04:37.380
but I feel like

01:04:37.380 --> 01:04:40.020
examining ourselves is sometimes

01:04:40.020 --> 01:04:42.330
the best thing to do.

01:04:42.330 --> 01:04:45.210
And if you feel like it is
impossible without burning out,

01:04:45.210 --> 01:04:47.010
it probably is.

01:04:47.010 --> 01:04:49.410
But that doesn't mean you can't get help

01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:51.273
to find make some choices.

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:55.090
But it's back to that trapped feeling,

01:04:56.438 --> 01:05:00.573
and the drowning feeling
that becomes terrifying.

01:05:02.371 --> 01:05:06.960
And to go back to having somebody
walk you through and say,

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:09.750
if you are feeling trapped and burnt out,

01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:12.640
let's come up with seven possible ways

01:05:13.530 --> 01:05:16.050
that that could maybe
be a little different,

01:05:16.050 --> 01:05:18.540
and maybe one of those
will be worth trying.

01:05:18.540 --> 01:05:20.490
Not just one way.

01:05:20.490 --> 01:05:22.410
You quit and go to an island.

01:05:22.410 --> 01:05:23.853
It was kind of like,

01:05:23.853 --> 01:05:27.363
that is an option maybe,

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:30.130
for a lot of people, not an option.

01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:33.720
But I think it's so much about perspective

01:05:33.720 --> 01:05:36.180
and I think, I'll be curious
what Dr. Ireland says

01:05:36.180 --> 01:05:37.150
and I think that

01:05:38.580 --> 01:05:42.389
if you have belief in a certain faith

01:05:42.389 --> 01:05:45.750
or even if you don't believe
in a certain religion,

01:05:45.750 --> 01:05:49.390
but whatever your beliefs are about

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:52.680
what is valued, what's important,

01:05:52.680 --> 01:05:55.140
what those communities can do for you

01:05:55.140 --> 01:05:57.093
is to give perspective.

01:05:58.320 --> 01:05:59.670
And I think at the end of the day,

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:02.430
that's often what we're most afraid of,

01:06:02.430 --> 01:06:04.230
but we're most seeking.

01:06:04.230 --> 01:06:05.820
Is just a different perspective

01:06:05.820 --> 01:06:08.170
'cause we're trapped in
our own head and heart.

01:06:09.630 --> 01:06:10.930
- I hundred percent agree.

01:06:12.375 --> 01:06:14.520
I think that's a really helpful answer

01:06:14.520 --> 01:06:16.200
even if it feels straightforward just,

01:06:16.200 --> 01:06:18.090
and I think, as people listen to this,

01:06:18.090 --> 01:06:19.860
they will have that experience

01:06:19.860 --> 01:06:20.850
of what you just mentioned too.

01:06:20.850 --> 01:06:21.930
Just hearing it,

01:06:21.930 --> 01:06:23.520
even though you're not speaking with them

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:24.570
in direct conversations,

01:06:24.570 --> 01:06:25.890
just having that reflective moment

01:06:25.890 --> 01:06:27.870
I think will be really important.

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:29.280
- so my husband does for me,

01:06:29.280 --> 01:06:31.473
I always say he's my best partner because,

01:06:32.880 --> 01:06:35.310
and I know we're ending up so I'll just,

01:06:35.310 --> 01:06:37.098
I'll tell a little funny anecdote.

01:06:37.098 --> 01:06:40.170
So I'll come home sometimes and

01:06:40.170 --> 01:06:42.480
like many I'll complain about everything

01:06:42.480 --> 01:06:44.040
that everybody's asked me to do

01:06:44.040 --> 01:06:45.750
and how things are unfair

01:06:45.750 --> 01:06:48.420
and I'm being blocked off everywhere.

01:06:48.420 --> 01:06:50.730
Everything I try to do
is blocked by someone

01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:52.200
or the system and you know,

01:06:52.200 --> 01:06:53.460
we've all been there.

01:06:53.460 --> 01:06:54.813
The world's against us.

01:06:55.770 --> 01:06:56.880
And I'll say, I can't, you know,

01:06:56.880 --> 01:06:59.442
I don't know if I can do
all this work in this job.

01:06:59.442 --> 01:07:03.570
And he'll listen and he'll
say, that sounds hard.

01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:05.853
And then he'll say, let me remind you,

01:07:07.080 --> 01:07:10.770
every day, you get to go to a job

01:07:10.770 --> 01:07:15.000
that you feel like is making
a difference in the world.

01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:17.100
That people listen to you

01:07:17.100 --> 01:07:20.010
and sometimes think you have good ideas,

01:07:20.010 --> 01:07:21.810
that you have some power,

01:07:21.810 --> 01:07:26.011
it's not as much as you
want, but you have privilege.

01:07:26.011 --> 01:07:29.340
And he'll just run the list and he'll say,

01:07:29.340 --> 01:07:30.510
and of course in doing it,

01:07:30.510 --> 01:07:32.280
he's not trying to not validate it,

01:07:32.280 --> 01:07:34.383
he's trying to just give some perspective.

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:36.567
And and I go, you are,

01:07:36.567 --> 01:07:40.410
and I always say, yes,
I'm still frustrated,

01:07:40.410 --> 01:07:42.033
but yeah, I forgot about that.

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:47.070
And so for me, and that
doesn't work for everyone

01:07:47.070 --> 01:07:48.390
and I know that can be triggering

01:07:48.390 --> 01:07:49.980
and obviously not everyone feels

01:07:49.980 --> 01:07:51.000
like someone's gonna be able to say,

01:07:51.000 --> 01:07:52.050
and here's all your privilege,

01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:55.200
because not everybody goes to
a job where they feel heard

01:07:55.200 --> 01:07:57.750
and that the work they do
is meaningful and valued.

01:07:58.590 --> 01:08:01.470
But for most faculty,

01:08:01.470 --> 01:08:03.480
to get to where they are,

01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:05.730
you have been hired because people believe

01:08:05.730 --> 01:08:07.413
you have something to contribute.

01:08:08.310 --> 01:08:10.260
You have taken that job because you feel

01:08:10.260 --> 01:08:11.730
like you have something to give

01:08:11.730 --> 01:08:13.950
that's gonna change the world,

01:08:13.950 --> 01:08:16.320
that's gonna have impact.

01:08:16.320 --> 01:08:19.530
And that alone is an amazing opportunity.

01:08:19.530 --> 01:08:24.530
Yeah, the system stinks
and lots has to change

01:08:24.600 --> 01:08:27.390
and it's not fair and it's not equitable.

01:08:27.390 --> 01:08:31.023
But for the folks in the academy,

01:08:33.240 --> 01:08:36.180
there are certain beautiful things

01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:40.500
about the opportunities to
work in a space like that.

01:08:40.500 --> 01:08:42.000
It needs a lot of work,

01:08:42.000 --> 01:08:45.300
but it does offer a
pretty amazing opportunity

01:08:45.300 --> 01:08:46.440
to do that work.

01:08:46.440 --> 01:08:48.330
- Yeah. Highly agree.

01:08:48.330 --> 01:08:49.890
And again, just the
importance of community

01:08:49.890 --> 01:08:52.770
to be able to remind each other of that

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:53.850
the way your husband has done

01:08:53.850 --> 01:08:56.430
and to be able to celebrate those things

01:08:56.430 --> 01:08:57.263
as they happen.

01:08:57.263 --> 01:08:58.770
I mean, so many ways I've
seen communities rise.

01:08:58.770 --> 01:09:01.680
- Look at your job, you
get to do a podcast.

01:09:01.680 --> 01:09:04.620
I mean, it's amazing
that this gets to be part

01:09:04.620 --> 01:09:06.750
of your work and your mission

01:09:06.750 --> 01:09:08.253
is just an amazing thing.

01:09:09.273 --> 01:09:12.360
- I do feel honored,
blessed, and privileged

01:09:12.360 --> 01:09:13.440
in that sense as well.

01:09:13.440 --> 01:09:15.450
And it's such a reminder to me.

01:09:15.450 --> 01:09:16.590
I mean, everything you just shared

01:09:16.590 --> 01:09:18.930
as guests have come on
and shared that with me,

01:09:18.930 --> 01:09:21.210
as listeners have continued to share that,

01:09:21.210 --> 01:09:23.370
even people who've found this podcast

01:09:23.370 --> 01:09:24.203
through different means.

01:09:24.203 --> 01:09:26.280
I mean, even meeting with
someone this week who said,

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:28.380
oh, I just saw this pop
up into my YouTube feed

01:09:28.380 --> 01:09:30.480
and was able to share just the ways

01:09:30.480 --> 01:09:33.540
that the podcast has
really encouraged them.

01:09:33.540 --> 01:09:35.250
So again, as you've mentioned,

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:38.220
that privilege and that
perspective is huge.

01:09:38.220 --> 01:09:40.620
In some ways I feel like I've been,

01:09:40.620 --> 01:09:43.500
I've been using this
podcast to facilitate more

01:09:43.500 --> 01:09:46.093
of these conversations because
I come away so encouraged

01:09:46.093 --> 01:09:47.820
from these types of things as well.

01:09:47.820 --> 01:09:49.800
And for me, I would even say this past,

01:09:49.800 --> 01:09:51.630
I can't even track anymore,
it's been two years,

01:09:51.630 --> 01:09:53.430
almost two years in the podcast

01:09:53.430 --> 01:09:56.760
has been so helpful for
that perspective as well.

01:09:56.760 --> 01:09:58.980
And just that frequent reminder.

01:09:58.980 --> 01:10:00.900
And then again, seeing
people who are invested

01:10:00.900 --> 01:10:03.600
in so many different
areas and walks of life

01:10:03.600 --> 01:10:04.743
I think is really,

01:10:06.150 --> 01:10:07.110
just really, really touching.

01:10:07.110 --> 01:10:09.450
I don't even know if I can
put words into it per se

01:10:09.450 --> 01:10:11.520
as you're actually helping me reflect.

01:10:11.520 --> 01:10:13.410
And I think just projecting it forward,

01:10:13.410 --> 01:10:16.290
projecting it onto
listeners who are tuning in,

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:17.940
even for those for me of us

01:10:17.940 --> 01:10:19.560
who have felt feelings of burnout

01:10:19.560 --> 01:10:21.900
or getting too close, trying to pull back,

01:10:21.900 --> 01:10:23.760
I think the perspective is huge.

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:25.350
And again, it goes back
to what we talked about

01:10:25.350 --> 01:10:27.720
I think at the beginning of this recording

01:10:27.720 --> 01:10:31.230
and just the place that we
both seem to be at this moment.

01:10:31.230 --> 01:10:33.300
Being able to be excited and reflective

01:10:33.300 --> 01:10:35.340
and being able to hold onto that

01:10:35.340 --> 01:10:37.470
even when the busyness comes.

01:10:37.470 --> 01:10:40.143
So I appreciate you sharing that story

01:10:40.143 --> 01:10:41.598
from your husband that, I mean

01:10:41.598 --> 01:10:43.710
that's part I'm gonna go
back and listen to again,

01:10:43.710 --> 01:10:44.760
just the way that he,

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:48.222
I like how he said that people
sometimes listen to you.

01:10:48.222 --> 01:10:49.887
'Cause I think, I mean
that's part of it as well

01:10:49.887 --> 01:10:51.866
that's that mixture.

